eat","mistake","great","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","good","good","brilliant","excellent","great","best","best","best","best","excellent","best","excellent","brilliant","best","best"],"moments":[{"ply":43,"name":"22.Nxd4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"22.Nxd4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the knight on d4.","Protects the c2 pawn, which was hanging.","The next best move, 22.Bd3, would drop White's winning chances from 61% to 46% (−0.39).","22.Nxd4 stops …Nxc2.","Black was threatening …Nxc2, winning a pawn. Nxd4 takes care of it.","Trades knights."]},{"ply":49,"name":"25.Nb3","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"25.Nb3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the rook on d4, which is undefended.","Shores up the c5 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 25.f4, would drop White's winning chances from 68% to 38% (−1.28).","25.Nb3 threatens Nxd4, winning a rook, and stops …Re1.","It threatens Nxd4, winning a rook, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black was threatening …Re1, winning a bishop for a rook, picking it up with 26…Rxf1+. Nb3 takes care of it."]},{"ply":69,"name":"35.b5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"35.b5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Pushes the passed pawn to b5.","Shores up the c6 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 35.Ba6, would drop White's winning chances from 79% to 65% (+1.63).","35.b5 prepares Ba5 and pushes the passed pawn to b5.","It prepares Ba5: the pawn clears the way.","Black answers 35…f4, pushing the pawn storm towards the king."]},{"ply":70,"name":"35…Bc4","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"35…Bc4 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 23% to 12%.","points":["White answers 36.Ba5, which attacks the knight on b6, which is undefended.","Better was 35…f4 (+3.25), which pushes a pawn towards the White king, to pry open lines in front of it."],"better":"35…f4 pushes the pawn storm towards the king and gains space on the kingside. Engine line: 36.Ba5 Nc4 37.Bb4 Nb6 38.h4 Nc8 (+3.25)."},{"ply":71,"name":"36.Ba5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"36.Ba5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the knight on b6, which is undefended.","The next best move, 36.Bb4, would drop White's winning chances from 88% to 70% (+2.34).","36.Ba5 attacks the knight on b6, which is undefended.","Black answers 36…Bxf1, taking the bishop on f1, leaving White with no bishop pair.","After 36…Bxf1 37.Kxf1 Nc4 38.Bc7 Rd5 39.b6 Rxd4 40.b7, the bishops are traded and White is a piece down (+4.02)."]},{"ply":79,"name":"40.b6","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"40.b6!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Leaves the knight on d4 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: Black's best reply is 40…Nxb6 41.Bxb6 Ke7 42.c7 instead (the engine rates it +6.21).","Pushes the passed pawn to b6, close to promotion.","40.b6 wins a knight for a pawn, picking it up with 41.Bxb6, and stops …Rxd4.","Sets up a sequence and wins a knight for a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 41.Bxb6.","Black was threatening …Rxd4, winning a knight. b6 takes care of it."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"40.b6!! leaves the knight on d4 hanging. Taking it lets White's pawn become a queen."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"The knight on d4 is attacked by the rook on d5, and 40.b6 doesn't save it. That's a knight on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the knight","text":"40…Rxd4 is met by 41.b7, pushing the passed pawn to b7. Then 42.b8=Q makes a new queen, and 43.Bd8 threatens Qa7+, winning a rook. White ends up a rook's worth of material ahead: White is winning (+6.22)."},{"title":"Black's best try","text":"Black's best is to leave the knight alone and play 40…Nxb6. White answers 41.Bxb6, taking the knight on b6. Then 42.c7 threatens c8=Q, winning the exchange, and 44.Ke2 attacks the rook on d1, which is undefended. White ends up two pawns ahead: White is winning (+6.21)."},{"title":"Why not save the knight first?","text":"Saving it with 40.Nxf5, the best way to do that, is clearly weaker (0.00 against +6.21). Black answers 40…Rxf5, taking the knight on f5 and threatening …Rxb5, winning a pawn. That would leave White level."}]},{"ply":81,"name":"41.b7","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"41.b7! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Pushes the passed pawn to b7, close to promotion.","The next best move, 41.h3, would drop White's winning chances from 92% to 34% (−1.78).","41.b7 stops …Na5.","Black was threatening …Na5, winning the queen for a knight, picking it up with 42…Nxc6. b7 takes care of it.","Black answers 41…Rd1+, joining the attack on the king.","After 41…Rd1+ 42.Kf2 Rb1 43.Bf4 Kf7 44.c7 Rxb7 45.c8=Q, the pawn promotes on c8 and White comes out 7 points of material up (+6.63)."]},{"ply":89,"name":"45.c7","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"45.c7!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Leaves the bishop on f4 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 45…gxf4+ 46.Kxf4 Ke7 47.b8=Q the engine rates it +8.31.","Pushes the passed pawn to c7, close to promotion.","45.c7 threatens c8=Q+, winning a knight, picking it up with 46.Bxg5+.","It threatens c8=Q+, winning a knight, picking it up with 46.Bxg5+, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black answers 45…gxf4+, winning a bishop for a pawn."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"45.c7!! leaves the bishop on f4 hanging. Taking it lets White's pawn become a queen."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"The bishop on f4 is attacked by the g5 pawn, and 45.c7 doesn't save it. That's a bishop on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It threatens c8=Q+, winning a knight."},{"title":"If Black takes the bishop","text":"45…gxf4+ is met by 46.Kxf4, taking the f4 pawn. Then 47.b8=Q makes a new queen, and 48.c8=Q+ makes a new queen with check. White ends up about a queen's worth of material ahead: White is winning (+7.60). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Why not save the bishop first?","text":"Saving it with 45.Be5, the best way to do that, is clearly weaker (+4.89 against +8.31). Black answers 45…Nxe5, taking the bishop on e5 and threatening …Nxc6, winning a pawn. White would still be winning, but far less than after 45.c7."}]}],"opening":{"name":"Italian Game: Two Knights Defense, Polerio Defense, Bishop Check Line","eco":"C58","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 12.Rxe5: 23 moves in all, 12 by White and 11 by Black.","Theory runs out after 12.Rxe5: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}